From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Eugene Feinberg <uge@persimmons.ai>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 20:28:42 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 02/37] LLHD Deseq: recognise triggers behind a concatenated
 observed value

Deseq maps a clocked process to a register by finding its triggers among the
wait's observed values. When any observed value is not i1 it takes a
'projected clock' path that looks for i1 destination operands which are
PROJECTIONS (extracts) of an observed value. Two gaps in that path:

1. It drops the directly observed i1 values entirely. An
   always @(posedge clk or negedge rst_b) whose clock arrives concatenated
   still observes rst_b plainly, and losing it leaves too few triggers.

2. The observed aggregate may be BUILT FROM the destination operands rather
   than them being projections of it. OpenC910 gates each FIFO entry with its
   own clock and the frontend observes them concatenated:

     %3 = comb.concat %clk_out_1, %clk_out_0 : i1, i1
     llhd.wait yield (...), (%3, %rst_b : i2, i1), ^bb2(%clk_out_0, %rst_b)

   Observing the aggregate means a change to any operand wakes the process, so
   an operand passed on to the body is as legitimate a trigger as a projection
   would be.

Both are now handled. On OpenC910 this takes unlowered processes from 190 to
42 and creates 175 real registers (seq.firreg 4607 -> 4782); combined with the
LowerProcesses change the design goes from 1441 unlowered processes and 10374
residual llhd ops to 42 and 903.

ibex is unaffected: its core.mlir, core_deagg.mlir and struct.mlir are all
byte-identical, and so is the synthesised netlist.
---
 lib/Dialect/LLHD/Transforms/Deseq.cpp | 30 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 30 insertions(+)

diff --git a/lib/Dialect/LLHD/Transforms/Deseq.cpp b/lib/Dialect/LLHD/Transforms/Deseq.cpp
index 681b694e5..8d1cde735 100644
--- a/lib/Dialect/LLHD/Transforms/Deseq.cpp
+++ b/lib/Dialect/LLHD/Transforms/Deseq.cpp
@@ -444,6 +444,15 @@ bool Deseq::analyzeProcess() {
     for (auto value : wait.getObserved())
       triggers.insert(getValueField(value));
   } else {
+    // A non-i1 observed value does not stop the directly observed i1 values
+    // from being triggers in their own right -- an `always @(posedge clk or
+    // negedge rst_b)` whose clock arrives concatenated still observes rst_b
+    // plainly. Without this the reset is dropped and the process ends up with
+    // too few triggers to lower.
+    for (auto value : wait.getObserved())
+      if (value.getType().isSignlessInteger(1))
+        triggers.insert(getValueField(value));
+
     // Projected clock case: find i1 dest operands that are projections of
     // observed values. These become our triggers.
     for (auto operand : wait.getDestOperands()) {
@@ -453,6 +462,27 @@ bool Deseq::analyzeProcess() {
       // Check if this is a projection (fieldID != 0) of an observed value.
       if (vf.fieldID != 0 && llvm::is_contained(wait.getObserved(), vf.value)) {
         triggers.insert(vf);
+        continue;
+      }
+      // Mirror of the projection case: the observed value may instead be an
+      // aggregate BUILT FROM this operand rather than the operand being a
+      // projection of it. OpenC910 gates each FIFO entry with its own clock
+      // and the frontend observes them concatenated:
+      //
+      //   %3 = comb.concat %clk_out_1, %clk_out_0 : i1, i1
+      //   llhd.wait yield (...), (%3, %rst_b : i2, i1), ^bb2(%clk_out_0, %rst_b)
+      //
+      // Observing the aggregate means a change to any of its operands wakes
+      // the process, so an operand passed on to the body is as legitimate a
+      // trigger as a projection would be.
+      for (auto observed : wait.getObserved()) {
+        auto *defOp = observed.getDefiningOp();
+        if (!defOp || !isa<comb::ConcatOp, hw::ArrayCreateOp>(defOp))
+          continue;
+        if (llvm::is_contained(defOp->getOperands(), operand)) {
+          triggers.insert(vf);
+          break;
+        }
       }
     }
   }
